        "reasoning": "The committed headline (A=38x/40x, commit 5309002, t_acronym.py) rests on FRA vs head-ablation and content-suppress only — both non-selective by construction (head-ablation zeros the whole mechanism; content-suppress subtracts the 'O' unembedding everywhere). These break all acronyms, so any selective method beats them; the campaign's own t_acronym_patch.py header concedes they are not the strongest fair baseline. The strongest fair selective baseline (position attention-patch) is uncommitted, unrun, and its FRA comparison number is hardcoded rather than measured. Because the 3 collateral acronyms are independent forward passes, a position-patch on the Officer edge would also score ~0 collateral on them — so on the literal headline metric the fair baseline ties FRA, and the 38x is an artifact of the metric being defined over other prompts where any position-tied edit trivially doesn't fire. FRA's defensible advantage is therefore confined to TRANSFER (content-addressing finds Officer at a new position the naive patch misses), but (a) that is a different and smaller claim than A=38x, and (b) the patch's own oracle cell recovers transfer given the position, and (c) the truly strong fair baseline — a content-gated linear steer, which is content-addressed AND could transfer — was never tested, so we cannot yet attribute the transfer win to the bilinear QK structure rather than to content-addressing per se. This does not KILL the result (FRA's transfer behavior is real and the screen-predicted direction holds) but it is FATAL to the specific headline \"A=38x is a confirmed FRA win\": the headline overstates by comparing to strawmen, the fair-baseline cell is unmeasured, and the honest headline should be the transfer result — contingent on FRA also beating a content-gated linear steer. Verdict wounded, fatal to the headline framing, survivable as a transfer claim if the decisive 2x2 is run."
