        "reasoning": "The attack lands on strength/generality, not existence. Three things keep it from being fatal to \"a confirmed FRA win.\" (1) Framing: the campaign and CAMPAIGN_LOG explicitly position these as existence proofs (\"find PUBLISHED behaviors where FRA beats baselines\"), and the headline summary.md does NOT yet even mention acronyms — it has not been promoted to an external-validity claim, so there is no overclaim to kill yet. An existence proof needs one clean case, and the mechanism shown is genuine: t_acronym_patch.py demonstrates the content-addressed pair edit transfers to a position where the position-tied attention-patch cannot (the 2x2 logic is sound). (2) The most robust metric, LBNR-R, rests on a paper-level fact (Garcia-Carrasco report a clean 8-head circuit with NO backup recruitment), so it is the LEAST likely of the three to be a probe artifact — unlike IOI/binding which died precisely because backups appeared (R=-0.39, R=-0.89). (3) A>1 versus head-ablation is near-structural: head-ablation zeros heads shared by all acronyms, so its collateral is high by construction across any dataset. What genuinely wounds the claim: (a) zero dataset coverage despite the dataset being public and cheap; (b) the tokenization confound is real and untested (I verified 4/4 picks sit in the clean single-token bucket while ~43% of realistic words don't); (c) the A=38x ratio is measured at each method's default strength, NOT at matched on-target removal, so even the single-probe number is softer than \"matched\" implies. None of these invalidate \"a win exists,\" but they make \"A=38x\" and \"confirmed\" overstated until the dataset run lands. Verdict: wounded, not killed; not fatal; fix is one cheap, well-specified dataset experiment."
